<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catullus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 84–c. 54 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Gaius Valerius Catullus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Valerius Catullus was a Roman poet from Verona, active in the late Republic. His surviving collection of 116 poems includes love poems to a woman he called Lesbia, invective against public figures and learned poems modelled on Alexandrian Greek verse. He was the leading figure of the 'new poets' and strongly influenced later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> lyric and elegy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catullus.jpg" alt="Catullus" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Schorle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catull_Sirmione.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The poet <b>Catullus</b> mocked a Celtiberian named Egnatius for brushing his teeth with urine, a custom that the historian Diodorus Siculus also reported among the Celtiberians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Contact with the Greek world transformed Roman culture: Livius Andronicus translated the Odyssey into Latin around 240 BCE, Plautus and Terence adapted Greek comedy, Ennius wrote the epic Annales, and in the late Republic Cicero, Lucretius, <b>Catullus</b>, Sallust and Caesar created a classical Latin prose and poetry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Cicero’s speeches, letters and philosophical works set the standard for Latin prose; Julius Caesar wrote clear accounts of his own wars; Lucretius put Epicurean philosophy into verse; <b>Catullus</b> wrote lyric poetry of startling directness.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Verona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,10.99&amp;year=-65" aria-label="Verona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4811 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Catullus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5261 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5261 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,10.99&amp;year=-65">What was here in 65 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
